A||||16||fogglethorpe||Freedom!||||14||
Z||000000||fogglethorpe||11-14-2001||08:39 PM||||I have heard on the radio, and seen on tv, the liberation of most of Afghanistan. It is extremely moving to see those people literally dancing in the streets, especially in light of the fact that mere days ago, they were among the most brutally oppressed people on earth! One man said he had not heard music for six years. Others were having their Taliban-ordered beards shaven off in defiance. But, the most amazing thing was that the women threw off their veils without fear.<BR>I am realistic...there is still some territory in Taliban control, and eventually it will be taken by the opposition as well. And, at some point another government will be formed, and who knows what form it will take.<BR>I just hope and pray that those people will be able to continue to live in freedom.<BR>It makes me proud to be an American when I think of how we not only live in a free society, but we promote it everywhere else as well.||||reg||14
Z||000001||BC-GT||11-14-2001||10:08 PM||||Hmmm, I saw a few pictures in the Globe & Mail today where they(opposition soldiers) were dragging a Taliban soldier around, interrogated him then shot him in the head for reporters. Was a little shocked to say the least. Are these people going to be the new government? If so, good luck to the people there, they will need it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||loafer87gt||11-14-2001||10:36 PM||||I have a sinking feeling in my gut that after the Taliban is eliminated and a new government is established, backed by the NA, that they will eventually become just as abusive with their power as the Taliban. It is kinda Deja-vu of the old Afghanistan situation, we provided weapons to the Taliban, and they turned and used them against us. Time will tell if the same will apply to the members of the Northern alliance. <p>[This message has been edited by loafer87gt (edited 11-15-2001).]||||reg||1
