Z||000005||fogglethorpe||11-15-2001||07:01 AM||||I am also skeptical about the NA...they are hardened by years of war, and are far from being choirboys.<BR>But I do know this...when we won our Revolutionary war, no other nation stepped in to "nation build" on our behalf. We worked it out ourselves and formed the greatest and most free nation in history.<BR>I know this is a different situation, but if we try to rebuild Afghanistan, where does it end? We must remember our objectives.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fogglethorpe||11-15-2001||07:05 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BC-GT:<BR><B>Hmmm, I saw a few pictures in the Globe & Mail today where they(opposition soldiers) were dragging a Taliban soldier around, interrogated him then shot him in the head for reporters. Was a little shocked to say the least. Are these people going to be the new government? If so, good luck to the people there, they will need it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Are you sue they were NA soldiers, and not Afghan citizens? And can we really blame them?<BR> This is war...when the enemy is fleeing, they must be pursued and destroyed.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||SteveJ||11-15-2001||09:15 AM||||More respect for the NA, please. They maintained a standing army in the face of an obviously hopeless cause. At least it was until we showed up. Remember, please, they are the reason we won't see many body bags. As a Vet I dislike body bags. The NA is the reason we can fight this with just special forces.<BR>Shooting prisoners is not good and he may stand trial for it. The morality of our age considers it a crime against humanity. <BR>There was that photo of the South Vietnamese officer shooting his prisoner in the head. The photo became quite famous. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||11-15-2001||09:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BC-GT:<BR><B>Hmmm, I saw a few pictures in the Globe & Mail today where they(opposition soldiers) were dragging a Taliban soldier around, interrogated him then shot him in the head for reporters. Was a little shocked to say the least. Are these people going to be the new government? If so, good luck to the people there, they will need it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Even in the most "civilized" wars, monstrosities will happen. A war without senseless, brutal killing does not exist. Yes, we all remember the South Vietnamese officer that was shot in the head. We all remember the Vietnamese girl burnt by Napalm. We all remember the terrible images of dead children. But even worse thing happen. In any war. And there's no <I>innocent</I> party. It's a gut reaction when you have lived in incredible fear for such a long time. You want to get even, and you want to get even <B>now</B>.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Voytek||11-15-2001||10:40 AM||||Many people who've lived in Europe still hear stories of attrocities committed by the allies during liberation.<P>I know I've heard some horrible things that the Russian soldiers did to the Germans as they went through Germany in 1945.  I've also heard some HORRIBLE things the French soldiers did to German women before they left germany (this was first hand account).<P>Such is war.  Eventually right and wrong tend to blur.  I mean, if you're doing bad things to bad people, then it's OK, right? (sarcasm)<P>I'm confident that, ultimately, Afghanistan will be a much better place than it was under the Taliban.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000010||ray b||11-15-2001||01:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B> Even in the most "civilized" wars, monstrosities will happen. A war without senseless, brutal killing does not exist. Yes, we all remember the South Vietnamese officer that was shot in the head. We all remember the Vietnamese girl burnt by Napalm. We all remember the terrible images of dead children. But even worse thing happen. In any war. And there's no <I>innocent</I> party. It's a gut reaction when you have lived in incredible fear for such a long time. You want to get even, and you want to get even [b]now</B>.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>the viet general who shot the viet-cong in the PIC is in California now and was never tried as the cong he killed had killed the family of the general in the tet attack.<P>Many toryies were killed or fled in fear after our revolutionary war victory. most had all land and homes sezed by state's goverment.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||11-15-2001||06:00 PM||||It's going to be hectic... But I fear that the NA will prove to be a problem. The NA is made up of a bunch of warlords... So instead of one "Generalissimo" running the country, you have 12(?) of them... What Afghan needs is a government set up by the people. Doesn't need to be democracy either... If they *want* (for example) their King back, then the UN should back that. <P>What worries me is the reaction from the NA about NOT having any UN peace-keepers... That smells like trouble and ethnic cleansing.<P>But yes, the mad rush of CDs and Tapes being sold, dancing in the streets, and shaving of beards, That truly is a happy sight.  Has anyone seen any women tossing away the <I>burqas</I>? I'm not home enough to watch much, and that's something I'd really like to see  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>||||reg||10
